# THE ONLY IGNORE LIST A WORKSPACE HAS. The sandbox image installs it as the system-wide
# `core.excludesFile` (Dockerfile.sandbox), so it applies to every workspace repository whichever
# `.gitignore` the workspace carries, and a restore of an older bundle cannot bring back an older
# copy of it. A file named `.gitignore` cannot do this job: `az acr build` drops every one from the
# build context, so none reaches the image.
#
# A save also UNTRACKS whatever this list matches (`_COMMIT_SCRIPT`,
# `backend/src/services/build_sessions/snapshot.py`), so an entry added here leaves every
# workspace's history on its next commit. Never list app code, and never list a file the app's
# deploy pipeline needs from the snapshot (`backend/src/services/deploy` collects the bundle).

# Installed into the image, or written by the toolchain on every run. `next dev` and `next build`
# both regenerate `next-env.d.ts`.
/node_modules
/.next
/out
/build
/next-env.d.ts
npm-debug.log*
*.tsbuildinfo

# Values files. The platform injects the app's configuration as container environment variables;
# a values file an agent writes must never enter a saved bundle.
.env
.env.local
.env*.local

# The restore's transport file, written into the worktree and removed as the restore's last step.
/app.bundle*

# Served by the platform itself.
/instrumentation-client.ts

# NOT ignored, on purpose: /drizzle. The generated migration SQL (and its meta/ journal) is a
# versioned artifact, not build output: it is the only record of how the database reached its
# current shape. Ignoring it would make a snapshot restore come back with code that expects tables
# and a database that has none. Never add a `drizzle/` entry here.
